    "result_text": "All 3 tests pass and the build succeeds. The full test suite is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI changed telephone handling to store and compare numbers in **E.164 form**.\n\n**Normalization rule** (`OwnerRestControllerV1.normalizeTelephone`): strip spaces, dashes and brackets; keep a leading `+` and its country code when present, otherwise assume `+61` and drop a single leading `0` from the national digits; require 8\u201315 digits after the `+`, else throw `InvalidTelephoneException` (\u2192 400). E.g. `0412 345 678` \u2192 `+61412345678`, `+64 21 123 456` \u2192 `+6421123456`, `12` \u2192 rejected.\n\n**Files changed:**\n- `rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java` \u2014 rewrote `normalizeTelephone` for E.164; `rejectDuplicateTelephone` now compares E.164 values (normalizing each existing owner's stored number the same way, skipping any legacy value that can't be normalized); `updateOwner` now normalizes its telephone too.\n- `model/Owner.java` \u2014 replaced the `@Digits(integer=10)` + `^[0-9]{10}$` bean-validation constraints with an E.164 `@Pattern` (`^\\+[0-9]{8,15}$`), so the stored value passes JPA validation on persist.\n- `rest/advice/InvalidTelephoneException.java` and `ExceptionControllerAdvice.java` \u2014 updated messages/javadoc to describe the E.164 rule.\n- `resources/openapi.yml` \u2014 updated the `telephone` field description and example.\n\nThe `telephone` column is `VARCHAR(20)`, which comfortably holds the max 16-char E.164 value, so no schema change was needed.",
